  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 10fc3a025822d194aad4d77cfe8095cc, type: 3}
  m_Name: M4_KanBanMarket
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::MissionData
  missionID: 4
  missionName: The Tangled Marketplace
  isAdvancedMission: 1
  villagerComplaint:
  - Half the stalls sit empty by midday and buyers just walk off!
  - But the other half are drowning in stock that's gone bad before anyone bought
    it — nobody can tell what's actually needed until it's too late.
  actualRootCause: There's no pull-based restocking signal — merchants push large
    speculative orders instead of pulling exactly enough stock, exactly when it's
    actually needed.
  fiveWhys:
  - question: Why do some stalls sell out while others waste stock?
    correctAnswer: Stalls either run dry or end up overstocked.
    distractors:
    - Not enough buyers visit the market.
    - Buyers can't find some of the stalls.
    - The merchants are just lazy.
    hint: "Look at the stock itself — too little, or too much."
  - question: Why do stalls run dry or get overstocked?
    correctAnswer: Merchants restock by feel, not by what sells.
    distractors:
    - Suppliers deliver at random times.
    - Merchants don't care about their stock.
    - There are too few merchants.
    hint: How does a merchant decide when to reorder?
  - question: Why do merchants restock by feel?
    correctAnswer: Nothing signals when a stall is running low.
    distractors:
    - Merchants were never trained.
    - The market has no clock.
    - Buyers don't say what they want.
    hint: What would a merchant need to know to restock on time?
  - question: Why is there no such signal?
    correctAnswer: No stall has a set reorder point.
    distractors:
    - There's no room to store any tools.
    - Suppliers refuse small orders.
    - Merchants think bulk buying is cheaper.
    hint: Has anyone ever decided "restock at this level"?
  - question: Why has no one set a reorder point?
    correctAnswer: No one tracks stock against real demand.
    distractors:
    - The market never had a bad enough day.
    - No one here has run a business before.
    - No official is in charge of it.
    hint: What kind of system would fix this for good?
  trivialReflectionText: No matter how you adjusted the signals, they never quite
    held up — a stall would run dry one day and drown in stock the next. After one
    bad day too many, the merchants gave up on the system and went back to just
    restocking whatever looked empty, the way they always had.
  optimalReflectionText: Each stall now signals the moment it actually needs more
    — never empty, never overflowing. You barely had to watch over any of them;
    the market keeps itself running exactly as it should. Turns out the idea has
    a name — a Kanban system — and it was invented by watching supermarkets do
    exactly this.
  introObjective: The marketplace can't keep its shelves balanced — go see what's
    going wrong.
  trivialObjectives: []
  optimalObjectives:
  - Set reorder-point signals and test the day ({0}/{1} attempts used).
